,"children":[{"type":"text","value":"To find the p-value, we can either:","position":{"start":{"line":36,"column":1},"end":{"line":36,"column":1}},"key":"Ewpr4mOi8r"}],"key":"IYJHxlFpk5"},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"spread":false,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"use the (68% part of the) Standard Deviation Rule for the normal distribution, which tells us that the p-value is approximately 0.16 (since P(−1 < Z < 1) = 0.68, each tail beyond 1 standard deviation holds about 0.16), or","position":{"start":{"line":38,"column":1},"end":{"line":38,"column":1}},"key":"i82rRauJEA"}],"key":"Uk7MvBkYqq"}],"key":"op3YAng7KC"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"use a normal table, or","position":{"start":{"line":39,"column":1},"end":{"line":39,"column":1}},"key":"k6OcsOiTzU"}],"key":"ao7vmbN9CO"}],"key":"M4x65tRXWz"},{"type":"listItem","spread":true,"position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"carry out the test using statistical software. In this case, we get a p-value of 0.159.","position":{"start":{"line":40,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"FesCp6AQb4"}],"key":"wt0hetk2ph"}],"key":"O1TcwxurNk"}],"key":"JzOmteqQL7"}],"class":"tip","key":"MTgV0dkehf"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example 2: Concentration of a Chemical in a Drug","position":{"start":{"line":43,"column":1},"end":{"line":43,"column":1}},"key":"BiOLi4Beyv"}],"key":"zTnJUdQhA9"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"In the concentration level example, the test statistic was found to be z = −2.5. Since this is a two-sided test (","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"ibedTUU41Q"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"H_a: \\mu \\neq 250","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>a</mi></msub><mo>:</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo mathvariant=\"normal\">≠</mo><mn>250</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">H_a: \\mu \\neq 250</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8333em;vertical-align:-0.15em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.08125em;\">H</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t vlist-t2\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.1514em;\"><span style=\"top:-2.55em;margin-left:-0.0813em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span></span></span></span><span class=\"vlist-s\">​</span></span><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.15em;\"><span></span></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">:</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">μ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mrel\"><span class=\"mord vbox\"><span class=\"thinbox\"><span class=\"rlap\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"inner\"><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mrel\"></span></span></span><span class=\"fix\"></span></span></span></span></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span></span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">250</span></span></span></span>","key":"MyGcKoAEHR"},{"type":"text","value":"), the p-value is the combination of the two tail areas: the area to the left of −2.5 plus the area to the right of 2.5.","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"XP4GEDQbbG"}],"key":"iPCezD402Q"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The p-value is therefore 2 × P(Z > 2.5). We can either use a table or carry out the test using statistical software. In this case, we get a p-value of 0.012.","position":{"start":{"line":48,"column":1},"end":{"line":48,"column":1}},"key":"weV2P18ceX"}],"key":"xnSRUXVPl2"}],"class":"tip","key":"QCM4fnUu9T"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Check Your Understanding: Finding the P-value for a Mean","position":{"start":{"line":51,"column":1},"end":{"line":51,"column":1}},"key":"oWqZnPqjUY"}],"identifier":"check-your-understanding-finding-the-p-value-for-a-mean","label":"Check Your Understanding: Finding the P-value for a Mean","html_id":"check-your-understanding-finding-the-p-value-for-a-mean","implicit":true,"key":"IPG7N8ThAG"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-fa7b2b23175943b1af7aff3871fe44e5.mjs","model":{"question":"A z-test for μ with alternative Hₐ: μ < μ₀ produces z = −1.5. Which expression gives the p-value?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"P(Z ≤ −1.5)","feedback":"Correct! For a left-tailed test, the p-value is the area to the left of the observed test statistic: P(Z ≤ −1.5) ≈ 0.067."},{"correct":false,"text":"P(Z ≥ −1.5)","feedback":"The area to the right of −1.5 (≈ 0.933) would essentially measure agreement with H₀."},{"correct":false,"text":"2P(Z ≤ −1.5)","feedback":"Doubling is only for two-sided alternatives."}],"multi":false,"hint":"A \"less than\" alternative is left-tailed.","explanation":""},"key":"c7mkTOEc0p"},{"type":"anywidget","esm":"/textbook/build/quiz-widget-fa7b2b23175943b1af7aff3871fe44e5.mjs","model":{"question":"In example 2, the p-value was 0.012. Which is the correct interpretation?","choices":[{"correct":true,"text":"If μ were 250 ppm, the probability of a sample mean as far from 250 as 247 is (in either direction) would be 0.012","feedback":"Correct! If the mean concentration really were 250 ppm, there would be only a 0.012 probability of getting a sample mean at least 2.5 standard deviations away from 250 (in either direction)."},{"correct":false,"text":"There is a 1.2% chance that the mean concentration is 250 ppm","feedback":"The p-value is not the probability that H₀ is true."},{"correct":false,"text":"If μ were 250 ppm, the probability of a sample mean of 247 or lower would be 0.012","feedback":"Since the alternative is two-sided, \"as extreme\" includes both directions, not just below."}],"multi":false,"hint":"Condition on H₀ (μ = 250) being true, and remember the two-sided alternative.","explanation":""},"key":"lQbefip8pz"}],"key":"ShvNRynxH0"}],"key":"CDmzCe36Aw"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"Stating Hypotheses and Summarizing Data for a Mean","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-2-o","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"},"next":{"title":"Step 4: Drawing Conclusions for a Mean","url":"/pages/m15-hypothesis-testing-for-the-population-mean-4-o","group":"Section 12: Hypothesis Testing"}}},"domain":"http://localhost:3000"}